  <text>
    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="3.1">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:IVAs">IV As</ref> 3.1</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="3.1" corresp="#wit-q">Et qui eum firmabit uel suorum aliquem mittet ad eum (su. quenquam mittat ad illum), pecunie sue reus sit in omnibus que habebit.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#attenborough" n="3.1" corresp="#wit-q">Et qui eum firmabit vel suorum aliquem mittet et eum, pecuniæ suæ reus sit in omnibus quæ habebit.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#attenborough" corresp="#wit-q">And if anyone harbours him, or sends him to any of his men, he shall be liable to the confiscation of all his property.</ab>
        <note type="comparison" source="#liebermann" n="171"><ref target="clause:VAs_Prol-to-3">V As Pr. 3</ref></note>
        <note type="comparisonPrinted" source="#liebermann">Vgl. V As. Pr. 3</note>
        <note type="comparedBy" source="#wormald-ppmel" n="128"><ref target="clause:VIAs_Prolog">VI As Prol.–1.4</ref></note>
        <note type="editorial">Latin only; no German translation in Liebermann. Liebermann would probably supply ‘uel quenquam’ after ‘aliquem’ from V As Prol. 3.</note>
      </div>
    </body>
